    "content_text": "BASF at Fakuma 2026: Resilience, Sustainability, Performance: Why the Plastics Industry No Longer Has to Choose\nBASF is presenting new material solutions for food packaging,industrial applications, lightweight automotive design, and next-generation mobility. \nAdvanced Engineering Plastics are enabling safer, smarter, and moreconnected technologies, from by-wire vehicle architectures to AI datacenters and high-voltage electronics. \nThrough circular materials, digital sustainability tools, and productivity-enhancing plastics, BASF is helping customers improve efficiency,reduce environmental impact, and maintain high performance. \nLudwigshafen, 17. September 2026 - Geopolitical uncertainties, regulatory requirements, and increasing performance expectations are reshaping the plastics industry. At Fakuma 2026, BASF will demonstrate under the guiding theme #OurPlasticsJourney how the perceived trade-offs between sustainability, resilience, and performance can be overcome. At the heart of this approach is the conviction that modern plastics can only be future-ready if they deliver on all three dimensions simultaneously: outstanding technical performance, verifiable sustainability, and a resilient, reliable supply chain. Rather than accepting compromises, BASF is focusing on scalable solutions that help customers reduce risks, achieve emissions targets, and maintain the highest quality standards at the same time.\n“Today, the plastics industry needs more than good intentions; it needs credible proof points. With #OurPlasticsJourney, we demonstrate how performance, sustainability, and resilience come together in real-world applications to create measurable value for our customers,” says Alexander Weiser, Senior Vice President Performance Materials Europe.\nBASF will present these and many other topics at Fakuma 2026 in Hall 4, Booth B4-4303:\nMaterial Innovations for New Applications and Markets\nInnovative Ultramid ® H Enables New Solutions for Smoke-Permeable Artificial Casings \nBASF has launched Ultramid ® H33 L, the world’s first thermoplastic polyamide with high water permeability. The material combines strength with water and smoke permeability, enabling smoked sausage casings made entirely of polyamide for the first time. Its thin walls and easy processing make it an attractive alternative to collagen and cellulose.\nNew Ultramid ® A grade for industrial applications \nBASF expands its polyamide 66 portfolio with Ultramid ® A27AE1 L. The new product grade offers an improved Yellowness Index in comparison to the conventional product and consistently low moisture content, meeting the requirements of demanding industrial applications.\nUltramid ® Expand (PA) Unlocks New Design Freedom in Lightweight Engineering \nWith Ultramid ® Expand, BASF showcases the potential of particle foam for lightweight, crash-resistant components with high temperature resistance. The material opens up new possibilities for innovative automotive applications where lightweight design, performance, processability, and safety are equally essential.\nTPU Light Guides Enable Design Freedom in Automotive Lighting \nBASF is showcasing innovative light guide solutions based on transparent Elastollan ® TPU for automotive interior and exterior lighting applications. The material combines excellent optical performance with high flexibility, durability, and efficient processing. Its exceptional low-temperature flexibility and low brittleness help ensure reliable performance and passenger safety even under demanding climatic conditions, while its elastomeric properties help minimize squeaking and unwanted noise in integrated vehicle components.\nPlastics for Electrification and Digitalization\nHigh-Performance Engineering Plastics for the Mobility of Tomorrow \nBASF is advancing high-performance material solutions for safety-critical applications such as brake-by-wire systems, high-voltage connectors, and power electronics. New flame-retardant polyamide and PPA grades combine excellent electrical insulation with design freedom, high thermal resistance, and long-term reliability.\nThe future vehicle runs by wire \nBASF is showcasing the future of braking systems for software-defined vehicles. A new demonstrator for electromechanical brake-by-wire systems illustrates the transition from hydraulically dominated braking architectures to electronically controlled systems. The demonstrator features a fully plastic brake pedal with integrated sensors, high-performance domain or zone control units, and electromechanical actuators. By employing redundant by-wire components, these technologies provide a key foundation for automated driving, enhanced functional integration, future zonal vehicle architectures, and additional lightweighting potential. At the same time, they open up new application opportunities for engineering plastics in sensors, control units, actuators, and pedal systems.\nUltramid ® Advanced (PPA) for challenging E&E applications \nThe polyphthalamides from BASF enable dimensionally stable, durable components with excellent hydrolysis resistance as well as color-stable E&E parts like PCB and high-voltage connectors. They support enhanced electronic performance, particularly applications related to AI, data generation/storage and power supply.\nUltrason ® (PSU, PESU, PPSU) for transparency, AI electronics and food- contact parts \nUltrason ® (PSU, PESU, PPSU) can be used in E&E applications such as fiber optical connectors (FOC) for AI data centers, wire insulation/cable jacketing used in electric cars, highly transparent optical components, and food-contact materials. The latter comply with the requirements of EU Regulation 2024/3190, as they are manufactured without BPA and BPS.\nSustainability and Circular Economy in Practice\nTPU Upcycling for a Circular Economy \nAt Fakuma, BASF will showcase sustainable TPU solutions such as Elastollan ® RC. The portfolio uses recycled TPU materials from post-industrial and post-consumer sources and returns them to the value chain as high-quality materials. This helps reduce waste, conserve resources, and lower CO₂ emissions without compromising material performance.\nInnovations for Greater Sustainability Across the Value Chain \nThe portfolio includes additional solutions designed to improve sustainability and efficiency. These include\nthe BOOXit reusable transport box based on Ultramid ® B3K (PA6),\nnew Ultradur ® RC grades (PBT) containing up to 30% post-consumer PETfor automotive applications,\nand new Ultradur ® grades (PBT) for drinking water and food-contactapplications that meet current regulatory requirements.\nThe portfolio is complemented by the digital Catena-X application PACIFIC, which enables the exchange and calculation Product Carbon Footprint (PCF) data, as well as the transmission of company certificates through Company Certificate Management (CCM).\nProductivity and Energy Efficiency in Processing\nHigh Productivity Plus: Greater Efficiency with the Same Performance \nWith the continuous expansion of its High Productivity Plus (HPP) Engineering Plastics product line, BASF is consistently broadening its offering for demanding applications. In addition to highly efficient PA6 solutions that enable shorter cycle times and reduced energy consumption, the portfolio will be expanded to include further flame-retardant materials and high-performance PA66 grades. This gives customers additional options to meet increasing requirements for safety and performance while improving productivity. How HPP Engineering Plastics can help increase efficiency and sustainability in plastics processing will be presented by Andreas Nixdorf in his talk, “BASF’s High Productivity Plus Engineering Plastics: The Key to Efficient and Sustainable Production,” on October 13 at 12:20 p.m. at the Fakuma Exhibitor Forum.\nAbout BASF \nAt BASF, we create chemistry for a sustainable future. Our ambition: We want to be the preferred chemical company to enable our customers’ green transformation. We combine economic success with environmental protection and social responsibility. Around 95,000 employees in the BASF Group contribute to the success of our customers in nearly all sectors and almost every country in the world. Our portfolio comprises, as core businesses, the segments Chemicals, Materials, Industrial Solutions, and Nutrition & Care; our standalone businesses are bundled in the segments Surface Technologies and Agricultural Solutions. BASF generated sales of around €60 billion in 2025. BASF shares are traded on the stock exchange in Frankfurt (BAS) and as American Depositary Receipts (BASFY) in the United States. Further information at www.basf.com .\nP-26-153 \nDr. Julia Endres\n+49 1609 6775641 Send email \nNews Release\nPreview PDF (397.36 KB) \nInnovative Ultramid ® &nbsp;H enables smokability of sausages in artificial casings with subsequent drying.\nPhoto: BASF\nPreview JPG (2.87 MB) \nFor thermostat housings: Ultramid ® Advanced T1000 HR with high hydrolysis resistance.\nPhoto: BASF\nPreview JPG (1.08 MB) \nReliable performance - come rain, come shine: camera dome made of Ultrason ® S (PSU).\nPhoto: BASF\nPreview JPG (1.21 MB) \nThe by-wire demonstrator illustrates how electronically controlled braking and steering systems are paving the way for automated driving, zonal vehicle architectures\nPhoto: BASF\nPreview JPG (1.42 MB) \nLast Update September 17, 2026",
