    "content_text": "TOPPAN and Institute of Science Tokyo Begin Joint Research on Microvascular and Blood Flow Analysis\nAiming to apply blood flow analysis technology using high-precision image analysis to medicine, TOPPAN and Science Tokyo commence evaluation and verification of its clinical significance for surgical applications.\nTweet \r\nOverview of TOPPAN and Science Tokyo joint research project \r\nTokyo &ndash; July 30, 2026 &ndash; TOPPAN Inc. (TOPPAN), a TOPPAN Group company and wholly owned subsidiary of TOPPAN Holdings Inc. (TYO: 7911), will enter joint research from July 2026 with the Institute of Science Tokyo (Science Tokyo) on blood flow analysis technology to visualize blood flow dynamics in real-time using a surgical endoscope, with the purpose of clinical application. \nPrior to this joint research, TOPPAN has developed a blood flow analysis technology that uses high-precision image analysis and image processing technology to recognize red blood cells in capillaries via surgical endoscopic imagery. No additional equipment or contrast agents are required for this technology as it uses standard visible light imaging* 1 for image recognition of red blood cell circulation, facilitating analysis of the direction of capillary blood flow. \nAs part of this collaborative research, the evaluation and verification of blood flow analysis technology developed by TOPPAN will be carried out in cooperation with Dr. Taku Ito, Junior Associate Professor at Science Tokyo&rsquo;s Department of Otorhinolaryngology. Verification of the accuracy and stability of blood flow analysis using real surgery imagery, along with testing of clinical utility for use during surgery will be conducted. Moving forward verification will gradually target to other specialties. \nThrough this collaborative research with Science Tokyo, TOPPAN will contribute to improving the quality of medical care by developing a new technology for medical imaging that will facilitate early detection of bleeding and ischemia (reduced blood flow) during surgery.&nbsp;\nBackground to Joint Research\nGenerating visuals of blood flow during surgeries makes it possible to determine immediately whether tissue has survived or not, or has been revascularized. This is of paramount importance in preventing potential complications caused by excessive tissue removal or impaired blood flow. Fluorescent imaging and ultrasound equipment that are currently used to visually monitor blood flow not only require specialized pharmaceuticals or equipment incurring extra costs but also require personnel for operating the special equipment and analyzing the images. These extra requirements limit the widespread adoption of current methods across all surgical and endoscopic procedures. Consequently, there is a need for blood flow analysis tools accessible to small- and mid-sized hospitals and training centers. \nThe TOPPAN Group has designated healthcare as a strategic focus and aims to establish new business in this area. As part of this, TOPPAN is adapting color management technologies developed in its printing business for use in the healthcare domain, while also advancing business generation that leverages precision image analysis technology. \nThe Institute of Science Tokyo Hospital functions as a comprehensive medical facility with a wide range of clinical departments. Backed by an extensive track record in advanced, diverse surgical and endoscopic treatments, alongside a solid research foundation in cutting-edge video analysis and surgical assistance, the hospital is uniquely positioned to manage everything from clinical problem identification to technology development and societal implementation. \nIn collaboration with many of the various medical departments, this collaborative research will leverage both parties&rsquo; strengths in developing and verifying technology that can be applied in a wide range of surgical fields. \nUsing blood flow analysis technology, this project aims to improve the quality of medical care and reduce disparities, by contributing to medical support in regions or facilities experiencing limited resources.&nbsp;\nBlood Flow Analysis Technology\nThe blood flow analysis technology developed by TOPPAN uses precision image analysis and know-how based on experience in color correction via color management technologies. Precision image stabilization and a proprietary algorithm for difference extraction developed by TOPPAN across testing and quality control in the manufacturing process have been redesigned for medical use. This will make it possible to isolate and detect the movement of red blood cells in capillaries using images taken with standard surgery cameras or endoscopy cameras, and in doing so make visible the presence of or changes in blood flow by extracting subtle luminance variation (change in brightness) of the tissue surface. \nBenefits this technology is expected to bring about: \n(1) Eliminates the need for additional equipment and pharmaceuticals, achieving low cost and high versatility: Analyzing blood flow is possible using only existing camera footage, completely eliminating the need for specialized equipment or contrast agents. Its high versatility and low cost make the technology easy to implement and operate across a wide range of medical specialties and in small- to medium-sized hospitals. \n(2) Visualizes changes in microvessels: Supports the early detection of intraoperative bleeding and ischemia directly contributing to enhanced safety during surgery. \n(3) Application in training and surgical records: Video footage can be quantitatively evaluated, making it highly beneficial for intraoperative education and the standardization of surgical techniques.\nDetails of Joint Research\n1.Verification of technical capabilities \nThe research will evaluate the accuracy and visibility of blood flow visualization when applying the blood flow analysis technology to video footage captured with standard surgical and endoscopic cameras, while also verifying technical capabilities for noise reduction.\n2.Verification of clinical utility\nUsing test-videos of a variety of situations from real surgeries and medical examinations provided by the Institute of Science Tokyo Hospital, the clarity, speed of image processing, and doctor satisfaction levels of the blood flow analysis technology will be evaluated. Then, along with identifying potential issues when implemented, utility as a surgical procedure support technology will be verified. Verification will begin by using images from the Otorhinolaryngology Department&rsquo;s routine surgeries and medical examinations that are regularly carried out via monitor using an endoscope. Verification will then gradually expand to other medical specialties. \n-Verification Period \nThrough March 2028\nCollaborative Roles\nTOPPAN \n&bull; Provision of blood flow analysis technology and verification of accuracy and stability.\nScience Tokyo\n&bull; Provision of surgical videos for blood flow analysis technology from the Otorhinolaryngology Department for verification. \n&bull; Preparation/provision of proof-of-concept environment in Otorhinolaryngology and other medical departments. \n&bull; Assessing and advising on verifying the technology.\nFuture Goals\nScience Tokyo and TOPPAN share a common goal to use cutting-edge blood flow analysis technology to improve the safety of surgeries and to reduce disparity in the quality of medical care. Together, they will conduct multi-faceted clinical validations to confirm the technology&rsquo;s utility. \nTOPPAN aims to advance this medical support tool for otorhinolaryngology into a business by the end of fiscal 2028.&nbsp;\n*1 Visible light imaging\nVisible light consists of wavelengths between approximately 380 nm and 800 nm that are perceptible to the human eye. As objects can be detected and recognized by the light they reflect under standard lighting conditions, specialized light sources or sensors are unnecessary. This allows for simple, cost-effective image recognition. \r\nAbout the TOPPAN Group\nEstablished in Tokyo in 1900, the TOPPAN Group is a leading and diversified global provider committed to delivering sustainable, integrated solutions in fields including printing, communications, security, packaging, d&eacute;cor materials, electronics, and digital transformation. The TOPPAN Group&rsquo;s global team of more than 50,000 employees offers optimal solutions enabled by industry-leading expertise and technologies to address the diverse challenges of every business sector and society and contribute to the achievement of shared sustainability goals.\nhttps://www.holdings.toppan.com/en/ \nhttps://www.linkedin.com/company/toppan/ \nInquiries regarding Press \r\nInquiries regarding Products & Services \r\nRelated News\nR&D \n2024/02/27 \r\nTOPPAN Establishes Method of Quantifying Simple Qualitative Test Results through Digital Image Color Management Techniques \nTag\nPerformance Materials \nSustainability \nEvents / Exhibitions",
