    "content_text": "SUMITOMO CHEMICAL News Releases \ntag:www.sumitomo-chem.co.jp,2018-07-10:/english/news//30 \n2026-09-18T12:27:34Z \ntag:www.sumitomo-chem.co.jp,2018:/english/news//9839 \n2026-09-19T00:07:48Z \n2026-09-18T12:27:34Z \nSumitomo Chemical has signed a Memorandum of Understanding (“MoU”) with Tata Electronics Private Limited (“Tata Electronics”) to explore the possibility of collaborating to build a semiconductor supply chain in India. Under the MoU, Sumitomo Chemical will examine specific measures aimed at establishing a stable supply system for semiconductor materials, including the potential establishment of a semiconductor chemicals facility in India.\nThe Indian government is promoting the development of the semiconductor industry as a national strategic priority. Against this backdrop, Tata Electronics is advancing the construction of India’s first front-end semiconductor fabrication plant. As India’s markets for semiconductors and related materials are expected to grow, Sumitomo Chemical believes that this construction project being undertaken by Tata Electronics will help strengthen the foundation of the country’s semiconductor industry.\nThe Sumitomo Chemical Group has positioned its ICT & Mobility Solutions sector as one of its growth drivers and designated its semiconductor materials business as a priority area within the sector. The Group provides a broad range of products, including advanced photoresists and semiconductor chemicals. In particular, it has established a leading position in high-purity chemicals and earned the trust of advanced semiconductor manufacturers through its ultra-high-purity technologies, quality assurance systems underpinned by advanced analytical technologies, and global supply capabilities.\nThe Sumitomo Chemical Group has been expanding the global business footprint of its semiconductor chemicals business through various initiatives. These include growing its production capacity in Japan and South Korea, establishing a new base in Texas, the United States, in 2022, and acquiring Asia Union Electronic Chemical Corporation in 2026, which operates manufacturing facilities in Kaohsiung, Taiwan, and Nevada, the United States. In addition to expanding the scale of its business, the Group has commenced operations at Advanced High-Purity Chemical Centers in Japan and South Korea in 2026, where it is advancing the development of highly productive processes and recycling technologies, and other related technologies. Going forward, by exploring potential collaboration with Tata Electronics, the Group will accelerate its assessment of business opportunities in the Indian market.\nSumitomo Chemical Group hopes that, should the collaboration be realized, it will contribute to the development of India’s semiconductor ecosystem.\n]]>\ntag:www.sumitomo-chem.co.jp,2018:/english/news//9834 \n2026-09-10T00:46:20Z \n2026-09-14T00:54:12Z \nSumitomo Chemical is pleased to announce that it will participate in the international semiconductor exhibition SEMICON India 2026, to be held in New Delhi, India, from Thursday, September 17 to Saturday, September 19, 2026.\nThis will be Sumitomo Chemical’s second year as an exhibitor at SEMICON India, after participating in the event last year. At its exhibition booth, the Company will showcase a range of distinctive solutions offered by the Sumitomo Chemical Group, including photoresists and semiconductor chemicals used in semiconductor manufacturing processes. All of these products are essential for the formation of fine circuit patterns. The Group’s technological capabilities and advanced quality management systems, developed over many years, have earned high recognition from semiconductor manufacturers worldwide.\nThe government in India, where the semiconductor market is anticipated to expand in the future, has positioned the semiconductor industry as a key national strategic priority. It is working to build a semiconductor ecosystem in order to establish a resilient supply chain and technological foundation.\nThe Sumitomo Chemical Group will continue to contribute to the development of the semiconductor industry. At the same time, it will strive to achieve sustainable growth in the global semiconductor materials market, including India.\nOverview of SEMICON India 2026 \nDate: Thursday, September 17 to Saturday, September 19, 2026\nLocation: India International Convention & Expo Centre, New Delhi, India\nWebsite: https://www.semiconindia.org/ \nSumitomo Chemical booth number: 876&nbsp; SEMICON India 2026 - Event Map \n]]>\ntag:www.sumitomo-chem.co.jp,2018:/english/news//9837 \n2026-09-08T06:04:28Z \n2026-09-18T01:59:23Z \nSumitomo Chemical has newly designated twelve of the Sumitomo Chemical Group’s products and technologies as Sumika Sustainable Solutions. This initiative, which is now in its 11 th year, is one of the Company’s efforts aimed at achieving a sustainable society. With this latest addition, the total number of Sumika Sustainable Solutions products and technologies has reached 101. These designated products and technologies generated an annual sales revenue of 555.6 billion yen in fiscal 2025, accounting for 24% of the Group’s total sales revenue.\nIn this initiative, which was launched in 2016, Sumitomo Chemical has designated products and technologies of the Group that contribute to mitigating and adapting to climate change and reducing environmental impact as Sumika Sustainable Solutions. This designation process includes reviews and assessments by multiple external experts. The Company has set the target of generating annual sales revenue of 1.2 trillion yen from Sumika Sustainable Solutions by fiscal 2030.\nThe newly designated products and technologies include Accede ® , a chemical agent that thins out excess fruit on stone fruit trees, such as plum and peach trees; a technology enabling thin touch-screen devices, such as mobile devices and IT equipment, which helps to conserve resources and reduce greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions; Thermofil Circle ® series 15R recycled glass fiber polypropylene, a glass fiber-reinforced polypropylene (PP) material that delivers quality equivalent to virgin PP, despite being made from recycled PP; a construction waste biomass power generation technology, which uses construction waste and food scraps as fuel to stably and efficiently generate electricity; and CFP-TOMO ® , a tool that calculates the carbon footprint of products (CFP) and is capable of handling CFP calculations for chemical products with complex manufacturing processes. For further information about these products, please see the attachment. Each of these products and technologies are linked to specific Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), and are expected to contribute to their respective fields.\nIn addition, since 2023, Sumitomo Chemical has introduced a new indicator, Science Based Contributions (SBC), to demonstrate more clearly how much its products and technologies contribute to the achievement of carbon neutrality. SBC quantitatively and scientifically calculates and visualizes the amount of GHG emissions that have been reduced in society through the use of technologies and final products that have been carefully selected from those designated as Sumika Sustainable Solutions and are expected to reliably reduce GHG emissions. The SBC results for fiscal 2025 showed a reduction of 7.14 million tons in GHG emissions. The Company will utilize SBC as a key performance indicator as it works to enhance climate-related disclosures to stakeholders, and will continue to advance its initiatives toward global carbon neutrality.\nSumitomo Chemical will strive to become an Innovative Solution Provider that solves societal issues by leveraging innovative technologies. The Company remains committed to creating solutions that contribute to the achievement of a sustainable society.\nList of the products and technologies newly designated as Sumika Sustainable Solutions\nSolutions\n(Group companies shown in parentheses) \n◆:&nbsp;&nbsp;Features\n●: Contributions\nContributions\nto SDGs \nBarrix Catch TM\n(Barrix Agro Sciences)\n◆A behavioral science-based trap for fruit flies. Its proprietary active ingredient and Pathway-Block diffusion design evenly disperse pheromones over a wide area to lure adult male flies into the trap.\n●Contributes to the reduction of pesticide use and minimization of the impact on aquatic systems and ecosystems by reducing the frequency of insecticide spraying.\nAccede ®\n(Sumitomo Biorational, Sumitomo Chemical Latin America, Valent U.S.A., Valent Canada, Valent de Mexico, Philagro South Africa)\n◆The only chemical thinner for stone fruits. It stimulates ethylene production in plants, promoting a natural abscission of flowers and small fruits. It has low environmental impacts and is highly biodegradable in soil.\n●&nbsp;It can reduce manual labor significantly, as well as improve fruit quality and ensure an proportion of fruit.\nTempest ® E\n(Sumitomo Chemical Latin America)\n◆A selective herbicide for pastures. Its proprietary formulation enables it to be highly effective even in small amounts, helping reduce the frequency of additional spraying.\n●By decreasing the frequency of spraying, it helps minimize the use of packaging materials and generation of waste. It also reduces fuel consumption and the number of transport trips for spraying, thereby lowering GHG emissions.\nNatural Pyrethrins Dry Aerosols\n(Sumitomo Chemical Latin America, SC Environmental Science Co., Ltd.)\nFlowering pyret&nbsp; hrum&nbsp; &nbsp;in Kindred, Tasmania - Botanical&nbsp; Resources Australia \n◆&nbsp;Aerosol insecticides containing 100% plant-derived natural pyrethrins as active ingredients. Natural pyrethrins rapidly break down in the environment and leave little residue.\n●Their use of biomass-derived raw materials and their ability to break down help reduce the risk of residues and lessen environmental impact.\nPolarizing films for large Liquid crystal display\n(Sumika Technology Co., Ltd.)\n◆Developed a manufacturing technology to reduce the environmental impact of producing polarizing films for large liquid crystal display.\n●Contributes to reducing GHG emissions and complying with stricter PFAS regulations.\nThin touch-screen devices\n(Dongwoo Fine-Chem Co., Ltd.)\n◆&nbsp;Developed a manufacturing technology for mobile touch-screen devices that enables the use of thin glass with the aim of reducing the environmental impact of the production process.\n●&nbsp;Contributes to reducing materials, waste, and GHG emissions.\nHigh-purity hydrogen peroxide\n(Dongwoo Fine-Chem Co., Ltd.)\n◆Improved production efficiency for a semiconductor wafer surface cleaning agent by using proprietary technology.\n●&nbsp;Significantly reduces GHG emissions and also contributes to reducing materials and waste.\nSUMIPEX™ for unpainted pillar covers\n◆&nbsp;A PMMA based coloring material for automotive pillar covers. It is highly scratch-resistant and provides an alternative to hard-coated ABS, enabling paint-free pillar covers.\n●Reduces volatile organic compound emissions by eliminating the painting process and improves recyclability by using a single material (MMA resin).\nThermofil&nbsp;Circle ® series 15R recycled glass fiber Polypropylene\n(Sumika Polymer Compounds Europe)\n◆ A high-performance, glass fiber-reinforced recycled resin made primarily from recycled PP. It ensures stable quality and mechanical functionality.\n●&nbsp;Reduces GHG emissions and energy consumption compared with virgin materials. By recycling waste plastic, it contributes to achieving a recycling-oriented society.\nNOBLEN ® MEGURI ® 1418 T15 UV BLACK BL018\n&nbsp;(Sumika Polymer Compounds&nbsp;Europe)\n◆ A PP compound primarily made from recycled PP for automotive interior parts. It also resists UV radiation and scratches.\n●Reduces GHG emissions and energy consumption by using recycled PP.&nbsp;By recycling waste plastic, it contributes to achieving a recycling-oriented society.\nCFP-TOMO ® , a CFP calculation tool&nbsp;\n◆ A tool that calculates CFP and is capable of handling calculations for chemical products with complex manufacturing processes. Built on Microsoft Access and Excel, it uses cost accounting data to enable efficient and accurate CFP calculations.\n● By improving the transparency of CFP data, the tool supports effective GHG emissions reduction efforts and helps visualize emissions across the entire supply chain. The tool is provided free of charge to external users and is used by more than 100 companies.\nConstruction waste biomass power generation technology\n(Kawasaki Biomass Electric Power Co., Ltd., Sumitomo Joint Electric Power Co., Ltd., Sumikyo Engineering Co., Ltd.)\n◆ A biomass power generation technology that uses construction waste and food scraps as fuel. The technology ensures stable operation by incorporating measures that prevent foreign matter from entering the system and mitigate corrosion.\n●Contributes to the reduction of GHG emissions and waste and helps promote resource recycling.\n&nbsp; \nReference\nSumika Sustainable Solutions: Main Products and Technologies\nSumitomo Chemical Establishes New Indicator of Avoided GHG Emissions Achieved Through the Use of its Products and Technologies—Science Based Contributions, June 15, 2023\n]]>\ntag:www.sumitomo-chem.co.jp,2018:/english/news//9830 \n2026-09-02T00:51:36Z \n2026-09-02T01:55:19Z \nSumitomo Chemical has developed a technology that improves permeance while maintaining separation performance in ceramic separation membranes (microfiltration membranes). The technology uses the Company’s high-performance alumina developed for separation membranes (the “newly developed alumina”) to increase the density of through-pores (*1) in the membrane structure.\nThis technology offers a new solution to a trade-off in separation membranes, in which improving permeance can reduce separation performance.\nThe mechanism of this technology was demonstrated through joint research with Kobe University. The findings were published online in Journal of Membrane Science Letters , a scientific journal, on August 12, 2026.\nThe results of the study indicate that the newly developed alumina provides new guidance for developing high-performance separation membranes for applications that are prone to the adhesion and accumulation of contaminants. Such applications include food processing, biotechnology, fermentation, chemicals, and wastewater treatment. Going forward, Sumitomo Chemical will propose solutions that contribute to higher value-added products, improved filtration system performance, and more stable operations for membrane and filtration system manufacturers.\nSumitomo Chemical is committed to becoming a company that solves societal challenges through innovative technologies. It will continue to provide solutions to challenges across a wide range of industrial fields through innovative technologies.\nOverview of Research Results\nKey Findings\nJoint research with Kobe University demonstrated the following:\n・In separation membranes, even when the average pore size is comparable, differences in through-pore density affect permeance and the deterioration of filtration performance caused by contaminant adhesion and accumulation.\n・In membranes with a high through-pore density, permeate flow (*2) is distributed across a larger number of flow paths, reducing localized loading and thereby mitigating pore blockage and contaminant adhesion.\n・Enhancing the performance of separation membranes requires design considerations that extend beyond average pore size to include internal flow-path structures.\n・Owing to its properties, the high-performance alumina developed by Sumitomo Chemical for separation membranes is useful for increasing the through-pore density of membrane structures and contributing to their improved functionality.\nBackground\nCeramic separation membranes are used in a broad range of fields, including food processing, fermentation, biotechnology, chemicals, and wastewater treatment, because of their excellent mechanical strength, chemical resistance, and heat resistance properties. However, in applications involving suspensions, emulsions, and other fluids in which contaminants readily adhere to pores, membrane permeance tends to decline.\nConventionally, membrane performance has been primarily evaluated on the basis of average pore size, while the influence of membrane structure was considered limited. In practice, however, pore connectivity and the distribution of flow paths can substantially affect permeance and the behavior of contaminant adhesion within pores. This has created a need for structural indicators that go beyond average pore size alone.\nResearch Results\nIn joint research with Kobe University, Sumitomo Chemical compared the internal three-dimensional structures and filtration performance for yeast suspensions of multilayer α-alumina tubular membranes.\nDespite having nearly identical average pore sizes, membranes with high through-pore density that were made with the newly developed alumina distributed permeate flow across a greater number of flow paths. This suppressed the intrusion of contaminants into the membrane pores, lowered the likelihood of internal pore blockage, and reduced resistance caused by contaminant adhesion. As a result, the membranes with high through-pore density exhibited more than three times the steady-state permeance while maintaining comparable separation performance.\nThese findings demonstrate that ceramic-membrane performance is influenced not only by average pore size, but is also substantially affected by how flow is distributed inside the membrane. They also show that the newly developed alumina is a material that contributes to high permeance through structural control of the membrane interior.\nSocietal Significance\nFor membrane manufacturers, these findings provide valuable insights that can support the development of higher value-added and differentiated ceramic separation membranes. For filtration system manufacturers, they can lead to proposals for higher throughput, reduced cleaning frequency, and more stable operation.\nNext Steps\nBased on these findings, Sumitomo Chemical will further advance structural-control technologies using alumina. The Company also plans to enhance its collaborations with membrane and filtration system manufacturers.\nThe Company will also pursue broader application of its material solutions. This includes applications in fields such as food processing, fermentation, biotechnology, chemicals, and wastewater treatment, in which declining permeance due to contaminant adhesion and accumulation remains a challenge.\n(*1) The number of pores that pass through a membrane or porous material from one side to the other, per unit area. \n(*2) The flow of fluid that passes through a membrane or porous material to the opposite side. \nPublication Details\nPublished in: Journal of Membrane Science Letters\nTitle: Role of through-pore density in governing filtration performance and fouling of α-alumina microfiltration membranes\nAuthors: Hana Hosokawa, Tooru Kitagawa, Shuji Nakatsuka, Setsuaki Murakami, Hideto Matsuyama\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.memlet.2026.100124 \n]]>\ntag:www.sumitomo-chem.co.jp,2018:/english/news//9828 \n2026-09-01T04:08:28Z \n2026-09-01T04:09:20Z \ntag:www.sumitomo-chem.co.jp,2018:/english/news//9832 \n2026-09-01T00:36:43Z \n2026-09-09T04:30:36Z \nSumitomo Chemical and Gutenberg Co., Ltd. (“Gutenberg”), which is leading the development of high-speed 3D printers produced in Japan, have jointly developed a liquid crystal polymer (LCP) filament and molding technology for 3D printers that use the FFF method (*1) . The Company will accelerate the development of applications in next-generation fields, including functional components that contribute to advances in semiconductors and physical AI.\n3D-printed parts \nRobot hand housing \nIn recent years, materials capable of handling complex geometries, achieving weight reductions, and integrating parts have been receiving increased interest across advanced industrial fields. For this reason, design and manufacturing using 3D printers have attracted increasing attention. Amid this trend, there is a demand for not only freedom of design, but also for high functionality in resin materials themselves. LCP is a super engineering plastic that combines heat resistance, high strength, low dielectric constant and low dissipation factor, and excellent dimensional stability. As a material for 3D printers, however, its use has been limited due to difficulties in filament formation and molding.\nTo address these challenges, Sumitomo Chemical has developed a material design and filament formation technology for LCP suited to additive manufacturing. The Company also collaborated with Gutenberg, which develops, manufactures, and sells industrial 3D printers, to examine the molding process and establish a technology that enables stable molding while controlling the unique molecular orientation of LCP. This technology achieves high thermal conductivity and strength comparable to that of aluminum alloys (*2) , while also maintaining lightweight properties. This makes it possible to create complex shapes that are difficult to achieve with injection molding, while also meeting the needs of low-volume, high-mix production.\nGoing forward, Sumitomo Chemical will expand its efforts into a wide range of fields, including peripheral components for semiconductor manufacturing, humanoid robot-related components that are expected to be put into practical use as physical AI advances, the communications field, and next-generation mobility fields such as eVTOL (*3) . In addition, the Company will accelerate the development of new applications by advancing evaluations of its filament and molded products in collaboration with customers. The Company plans to begin providing samples of this filament in 2026, and it is also looking ahead to future expansion into the space and defense fields.\nSumitomo Chemical will continue to promote the provision of innovative materials and solutions that support next-generation manufacturing. In doing so, the Company will contribute to the creation of new value and the development of society and industry.\n(*1) A 3D printing method in which thermoplastic resin is melted, extruded from a nozzle, and stacked layer by layer. \n(*2) The Company evaluated the fracture strength of LCP and aluminum alloy under certain conditions using a test method compliant with standards set by the International Organization for Standardization. \n(*3) Electric Vertical Take-Off and Landing aircraft, commonly referred to as flying cars. \nReference\nRobot hand that can operate under high-temperature conditions (video) \nFilming support and robot design by HALMONIUM\nThis video was produced for demonstration purposes. The LCP robot hand shown in the video is not currently registered for food-grade applications. It may be used for food-related applications in the future if it were to obtain approval for such use.\n]]>\ntag:www.sumitomo-chem.co.jp,2018:/english/news//9822 \n2026-08-31T05:02:44Z \n2026-08-31T05:03:40Z \ntag:www.sumitomo-chem.co.jp,2018:/english/news//9820 \n2026-08-31T00:48:40Z \n2026-08-31T01:40:33Z \nThe Company will Establish a Supply System for Core Materials to Contribute to the Societal Implementation of Photonic-Electronic Convergence Technologies \nSumitomo Chemical has established a mass production system for 4-inch indium phosphide (InP) epitaxial wafers, a type of compound semiconductor, at its Ibaraki Works in Hitachi, Ibaraki Prefecture, and has begun selling these products. As one of the few epi houses in Japan capable of stably mass-producing InP epitaxial wafers, the Company will contribute to the societal implementation of photonic-electronic convergence * technologies that support next-generation digital infrastructure. The Company also aims to achieve sales of around 10 billion JPY from its InP epitaxial wafer product lineup by the mid",
