ated in urban areas, with urban populations\nexpected to rise by around 2.5 billion people between now\nand 2050. This trend is accompanied by a gradual increase\nin living standards.\nRESOURCE SCARCITY\nThe growth in the world population, increasing living standards\nand the rapid pace of industrialization are all driving an increase\nin the use of the world’s resources. Based on current conditions,\nthe consumption of raw materials could therefore triple by 2050.\nCLIMATE CHANGE\nUrbanization, industrialization and the increase in the number\nof cars and air travel all contribute to global warming\nand climate change.\nTo address these issues, the Paris Agreement aims to keep\nthe global temperature rise by the end of the century\nto below 2°C.\nNEW TECHNOLOGIES\nNew technologies, such as artifi cial intelligence,\nmaterial sciences and robotics are growing fast,\ncreating new commercial and industrial possibilities.\nTHE CHEMICAL INDUSTRY\nOF ITS INDUSTRIAL OPERATIONS.\nGlobal consequences\n• S trong demand for the construction and renovation of buildings\nand infrastructure\n• I ncreased demand for transportation, energy, water\nand services\n• C hanging lifestyles and consumer behavior\n• I ncreasing concern about housing quality, comfort\nand energy effi ciency\n• S carcity of non-renewable resources\n• I ncreasing diffi culties in accessing safe drinking water\n• I ncrease in the global temperature and its social\nand environmental consequences\n• I ncrease and intensifi cation of extreme weather events\n• N eed to speed up the transition to more sustainable lifestyles\nand economic models (decoupling)\n• C hanging lifestyles and consumer behavior (e.g. percentage\nof the population equipped with connected objects)\n• Signifi cant increase in available data\n• Increased production rates\nSeen by many as the “industry of industries”, the chemical Lastly, the chemical industry is highly\nindustry manufactures a wide range of products for other major fragmented, in terms of products (several\nindustries, including the construction, chemical, automotive, tens of thousands), end markets, and\ncoatings, electronics, energy, food and pharmaceutical industry players (the top ten competitors\nindustries. In 2017, it generated estimated sales of around have no more than a 10% share of\n€3,475 billion (1) worldwide. the global market (2)). This level of\nfragmentation offers opportunities\nThe chemical industry operates on a global scale and more particularly for consolidation in certain segments such\nin three regions: Europe, North America and Asia-Pacifi c. The latter as adhesives.\nhas seen its share increase continuously in recent years. It now\nrepresents around 62% of global production in value\n(1) Source: Cefi c Facts and Figures 2018. Europe\nterms (1) and is expected to continue to record the fastest growth over accounts for around 19% and North America\nthe coming years. for 15%.\n(2) Source: Arkema internal estimates.\nReference document 2018 - ARKEMA 8\nPROFILE, AMBITION AND STRATEGY\nAmbition and strategy\nOUR SOLUTIONS\nTO CURRENT AND FUTURE CHALLENGES\nKey challenges\nArkema contributes ARKEMA’S SOLUTIONS\nto address\nINCREASING URBANIZATION\n• Facilitate the transportation of energy and water, • Advanced materials for water ultrafi ltration (Kynar® PVDF) and transportation\nas well as access to high-quality water. (Rilsan® polyamides)\n• Treat domestic and industrial wastewater • Hydrogen peroxide and acrylics to disinfect water and treat wastewater\nand other waste.\n• Meet changing demand for consumer products. • Acrylics and adhesives for hygiene, thiochemicals for animal feed\nand advanced materials for sport, cosmetics and packaging\n• Improve thermal and acoustic insulation, comfort • Adhesives and sealants by Bostik for insulation and sealing\nand air quality in housing. and low-VOC coating resins for paints\nRESOURCE SCARCITY\n• Contribute to the development of new energies, • Advanced materials for new energies: Kynar® PVDF for batteries and solar power,\nsuch as lithium-ion batteries for electric vehicles, Apolhya® for solar power, Elium® recyclable resin for wind power\nphotovoltaics and wind power.\n• Promote eco-design: • Bio-based Rilsan® polyamides 11 and 10, made from castor oil plants\n- Develop products made from bio-based,\nrenewable or recycled raw materials • Kynar® and Kynar Aquatec® coatings for refl ective roofs and Kercoat® and Opticoat®\n- Extend products’ lifespan coatings for glass bottles\n- Recycle end-of-life products. • Elium® recyclable resin for composites and the Cecabase RT® additive for asphalt\n• Optimize the consumption of energy,\nraw materials and water.\nCLIMATE CHANGE\n• Make vehicles and aircraft lighter, • Lightweighting solutions:\nto limit their fuel consumption and reduce - Advanced materials used as substitutes for metal: Rilsan® HT, Kepstan® PEKK\ntheir CO2 emissions. and thermoplastic composites (Elium®)\n- Altuglas® ShieldUp as a substitute for glass\n- Adhesives for the assembly of metal parts, as a substitute for mechanical bonding\n• I mprove buildings’ energy performance • Thermal insulation solutions:\nto reduce energy, heating and air-conditioning - Adhesives and sealants by Bostik\nneeds.\n- Kynar® Aquatec coatings for refl ective roofs\n• R educe greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions • Development of HFO refrigerants with a very low global warming potential\nacross the whole value chain.\n• 2025 target to reduce GHG emissions by 50% compared with 2012\nNEW TECHNOLOGIES\n• Contribute to the development of new markets • Advanced materials for digital mobility devices (technical polymers for smartphones\n(e.g. consumer electronics, sensors). and tablets and Kynar® for batteries)\n• Speed up the expansion of new production • Full range of resins for 3D printing:\ntechnologies such as 3D printing. - Sartomer’s N3xtDimension® photocure resins, Kepstan® PEKK, Rilsan® polyamides\n- for all types of additive manufacturing\n• Leverage digital technologies to support • Digital plant thanks to the deployment of predictive maintenance tools\noperational and commercial excellence. and 3D and 4D technologies\n9 Reference document 2018 - ARKEMA\nPROFILE, AMBITION AND STRATEGY\nAmbition and strategy\nA BALANCED PORTFOLIO\nOF BUSINESSES\nTHE GROUP’S BUSINESS PORTFOLIO COMBINES STRONG POSITIONS\nIN SPECIALTY BUSINESSES, WITH HIGH GROWTH POTENTIAL, AND GLOBAL,\nCOMPETITIVE INTERMEDIATE PRODUCT LINES.\nTHIS COMPLEMENTARITY ENABLES ARKEMA TO ADAPT TO DIFFERENT ECONOMIC\nENVIRONMENTS, AS DEMONSTRATED BY THE STRONG INCREASE\nIN ITS RESULTS OVER THE PAST YEARS.\n70%\nSPECIALTIES INTERMEDIATES\nAdvanced materials PMMA\n(Technical Polymers and Fluorogases\nPerformance Additives)\nAdhesives Acrylics\n30%\nThiochemicals\nPerformance coatings\n(Coating resins and additives)\nHydrogen Peroxide\nAs a % of Group sales\nHigh value-added activities combining Global leading positions:\ndifferentiation and innovation: • Number 2 or 3 worldwide\n• High technological innovation • Cutting-edge proprietary technologies\n• Solutions tailored to customers’ specifi c needs • Global and competitive production footprint,\n• Long-term partnerships with major industrial players, with platforms in Europe, North America\nleaders in their fi eld and Asia\n• Well-known brands • Strong customer partnerships\n• Proprietary technologies\nHigh-growth niche markets: Businesses with high profi tability\n• Driven by sustainable megatrends: lightweight materials, but greater exposure to\nbio-based products, 3D printing, home effi ciency, supply/demand balances\nnew energies\n• Limited number of players\nLONG-TERM TARGET\nGlobally resilient businesses, More\nclose to end markets\nand particularly well-placed than 80\nto contribute to the world’s current %\nand future major challenges\nOF SALES GENERATED\nBY SPECIALTY\nBUSINESSES\nReference document 2018 - ARKEMA 10\nPROFILE, AMBITION AND STRATEGY\nAmbition and strategy\nSTRONG ASSETS\nARKEMA CAN LEVERAGE A SET OF SOLID ASSETS AND STRENGTHS TO ROLL OUT\nITS STRATEGY AND CARRY OUT ITS NUMEROUS PROJECTS, ENABLING THE GROUP\nTO STRENGTHEN ITS POSITION AMONG THE WORLD LEADERS\nIN SPECIALTY CHEMICALS.\nExtensive R&D capabilities\nEnabling us to launch new products, provide our\nIn the top 3 globally customers with the technical support they need, and\non 90% of total sales further improve the effi ciency\nof our manufacturing processes, thanks to:\nWell-known brands\n• t he expertise of our 1,600 researchers\nContributing at our 15 research centers worldwide;\nto customer loyalty.\n• a portfolio of close to 9,000 patents; and\n• 244 new patent applications\nStrong long-term customer\npartnerships\nWith leading industrial customers\nin areas such as 3D printing, composites,\nwater treatment and batteries.\nExperienced,\ncommitted teams\nWho contributed to shaping Arkema into\na leading industrial group, thanks to their ability:\n• to carry out complex industrial projects\nsuch as the construction of a thiochemicals platform\nin Malaysia, in a new country for the Group, with\na new partner and based on an innovative process;\n• to ensure smooth integration of major\nacquisitions such as Bostik; and\n• to adapt to the different macro-economic\nenvironments that Arkema has faced\nover the last 13 years, and to strongly improve\nits fi nancial performance.\nfi led in 2018.\nA competitive\nand global presence\nTo support our customers in their\ngeographical expansion thanks to:\n• a strong manufacturing footprint\nin Europe, North America and Asia;\n• proprietary manufacturing processes\nthat are technologically complex; and\n• signifi cant expertise in large-scale\ninvestment projects, with high effi ciency\nin terms of costs, timing and technical\nimplementation.\nA solid\nfi nancial structure\n• Excellent cash generation\nand a high EBITDA to cash\nconversion rate.\n• Tightly-controlled net debt,\nrepresenting, at end- 2018,\n0.7 times annual EBITDA\nand 20% gearing.\nGiving us the fi nancial\nfl exibility needed to carry out\nour ambitious investment\nand targeted acquisitions\npolicy while ensuring regular\ndividend growth.\n11 Reference document 2018 - ARKEMA\nPROFILE, AMBITION AND STRATEGY\nAmbition and strategy\nTHE GROUP’S AMBITION\nMEDIUM- AND LONG-TERM TARGETS\nARKEMA’S AMBITION IS TO CONSOLIDATE ITS POSITION\nAMONG THE GLOBAL LEADERS IN SPECIALTY CHEMICALS\nBY ACCELERATING THE DEVELOPMENT OF THESE BUSINESSES.\nFollowing the stock market listing in May 2006, Arkema started an in-depth transformation\nto reposition its portfolio of businesses toward a signifi cantly higher proportion of specialty\nchemicals, which are more resilient, close to end markets, and offer strong growth potential.\nAccelerating t",
