    "content_text": "U.S. Commerce Secretary tours Corning plant one year after Apple announcement \nU.S. Commerce Secretary tours Corning plant one year after Apple announcement \nBy Phil Pendleton \nPublished : Aug. 28, 2026 at 4:45 PM EDT \nShare \nAdd Us On Google Add as a preferred source on Google \nHARRODSBURG, Ky. (WKYT) - U.S. Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick joined Apple’s chief operating officer and others Friday to mark one year since Corning announced a deal to make all iPhone and Apple Watch glass at its Harrodsburg plant.\nCorning has operated in Harrodsburg for almost 75 years. Officials said the announcement made last year may have been one of the company’s biggest.\nCompany marks anniversary\nCorning CEO Wendell Weeks spoke at the event.\n“Today what we are also celebrating is the bright future of the Harrodsburg, Ky plant as we seek to triple our output,” Weeks said.\nLutnick joined Congressman Andy Barr and others to tour the Harrodsburg plant Friday.\n“The states that win have the red carpet out for business. Apple can choose wherever it wants to build,” Lutnick said.\nCommerce secretary praises workers\nLutnick praised the Corning employees for their skill and expertise in producing a product that will be shipped and used around the world.\n“That means the scale and the scope of their production must be the best in the world. The quality must be the best. Also the speed, because you have to sell a lot of watches and phones,” Lutnick said.\nOfficials said American production should lower costs for consumers because products do not have to be shipped overseas. Barr said it should also lessen supply chain concerns.\n“We don’t want to be sourcing the technology that we depend on for our livelihood for our work, for everything we do every day from those other countries,” Barr said.\nPolitical reaction\nBarr is also running for U.S. Senate. His Democratic challenger, Charles Booker, said they support Corning’s economic development but said Kentuckians deserve a policy that benefits them all the time, not just when it’s convenient for those in power.\nApple’s chief operating officer said the announcement is part of 3 million American jobs the company is supporting.\nOfficials said by the end of the year, every iPhone and Apple Watch will have glass made in Harrodsburg.\nCopyright 2026 WKYT. All rights reserved.",
