    "content_text": "Qualcomm seeks to make Vietnam third-largest global AI R&D hub \nBy\r\nQuang Minh, Minh Hue \r\nFri, August 28, 2026 | 1:30 pm GMT+7\nQualcomm views Vietnam as an increasingly important market and technology hub in Asia and is seeking to make the country the U.S. chipmaker’s third-largest artificial intelligence research and development center globally, said president and CEO Cristiano Amon.\nParty General Secretary and President To Lam (right) receives Qualcomm president and CEO Cristiano Amon in Hanoi on August 27, 2026. Photo courtesy of Vietnam News Agency.\nMeeting with Vietnam’s Party General Secretary and President To Lam in Hanoi on Thursday, Amon said in June 2025, Qualcomm officially launched a new AI R&D center in Vietnam, following its acquisition of the generative AI research unit of VinAI , a subsidiary of Vietnamese conglomerate Vingroup. It is one of Qualcomm’s three research centers outside the U.S.\nVietnam gains importance in Qualcomm’s Asia strategy \nThe Qualcomm leader said his visit came as the company steps up its strategy to develop new technologies, particularly AI, semiconductors and connected computing, while continuing to expand its R&D operations in Vietnam. \nAmon spoke highly of Vietnam’s vision and determination, as well as its strong policies to promote growth, science-technology, innovation, digital transformation, semiconductors and AI, and to build an economy increasingly based on knowledge and advanced technologies.\nHe said he was impressed by Vietnam’s notable development achievements in recent years, saying the results demonstrated the country’s growing appeal to the international business community. They also provide an important foundation for Vietnam to emerge as a high-tech manufacturing, research, innovation and digital economy hub with an increasingly important position in the region.\nWith more than $100 billion invested in R&D globally, Qualcomm seeks to leverage its core technology capabilities, research network and international partner ecosystem to expand cooperation in Vietnam in AI, semiconductors, 5G/6G, connected computing, and next-generation technology infrastructure.\nThe group expressed a desire to further expand its cooperation, investment and long-term presence in Vietnam, while strengthening coordination with Vietnamese agencies, businesses, research institutes and universities in research, high-quality talent training, technology transfer, and the development of next-generation technologies.\nQualcomm also seeks to contribute to the development of Vietnam’s semiconductor, AI and innovation ecosystems in the coming period.\nQualcomm urged to expand long-term investment in Vietnam \nAt the meeting, Party chief and President To Lam affirmed that Vietnam attaches importance to its relations with the U.S. and expressed satisfaction with the stable, positive and increasingly substantive development of bilateral ties, particularly since the two countries established a Comprehensive Strategic Partnership framework in 2023.\nHe emphasized that Vietnam is transitioning toward a self-strengthening, innovative, sustainable and integrated development model, identifying science-technology, innovation, digital transformation, and national AI as key drivers.\nVietnam prioritizes attracting investment linked to technology transfer and the development and training of high-quality human resources, he said, adding the country welcomes and will continue to create conditions for leading global technology groups, including U.S. companies, to invest, conduct R&A activities, and pursue long-term cooperation in Vietnam.\nTo Lam welcomed Amon’s return to Vietnam and highly valued Qualcomm’s expansion of operations, investment in R&D, support for innovation and development of Vietnam’s technology ecosystem over more than 20 years. This includes the establishment of an R&D center in Hanoi and cooperation with leading Vietnamese technology companies such as military-run Viettel and VinSmart, another member of Vingroup.\nQualcomm has positioned Vietnam highly in its development strategy in Asia, with a focus on making the country an important R&D center within its global network. The Vietnamese leader said the direction was consistent with efforts to strengthen science, technology and innovation cooperation between Vietnam and the U.S. under their Comprehensive Strategic Partnership.\nHe urged Qualcomm to continue expanding long-term investment in Vietnam in areas where the group has strengths, including AI, semiconductors, robotics, 5G/6G, data centers, and new-generation connectivity technologies.\nQualcomm was also urged to strengthen technology transfer and share management expertise, expand cooperation with domestic businesses, universities and research institutes, and support Vietnamese companies in enhancing their technological capabilities and participating more deeply in global technology value and supply chains.\nTags:\nVietnam \nQualcomm \nthird-largest global AI R&D hub \nUS tech firm \nAI \npresident and CEO Cristiano Amon \nComments (\r\n0)\r\nLatest | Most liked \r\nTags:\nVietnam \nQualcomm \nthird-largest global AI R&D hub \nUS tech firm \nAI \npresident and CEO Cristiano Amon \nRelated News \r\nViettel becomes Qualcomm's first global partner in early 6G platform program \r\nCompanies \r\nVNPT, Qualcomm debut Vietnam’s 1st original design-based excellence center \r\nCompanies \r\nQualcomm deepens Vietnam commitment as key global hub \r\nCompanies \r\nVingroup earns $67 mln from selling 65% of Movian AI to Qualcomm \r\nCompanies \r\nRead More \r\nQualcomm seeks to make Vietnam third-largest global AI R&D hub \r\nQualcomm views Vietnam as an increasingly important market and technology hub in Asia and is seeking to make the country the U.S. chipmaker’s third-largest artificial intelligence research and development center globally, said president and CEO Cristiano Amon. \nIndustries \r\n-\r\nFri, August 28, 2026 | 1:30 pm GMT+7\r\nPrudential Vietnam’s H1 profit nearly quadruples as investment income offsets insurance loss \r\nPrudential Vietnam Life Insurance Co. reported a sharp increase in net profit for the first half of 2026, driven largely by higher financial income even as its core insurance business remained under pressure. \nCompanies \r\n-\r\nFri, August 28, 2026 | 12:06 pm GMT+7\r\nVietnam resort real estate enters new phase \r\nVietnam's resort real estate market is entering a new phase of development, with legal transparency, product quality, operational capabilities, and actual performance increasingly becoming key factors determining a project's appeal, experts said. \nReal Estate \r\n-\r\nFri, August 28, 2026 | 9:26 am GMT+7\r\nForeign investors post five straight sessions of net buying \r\nThe VN-Index extended its winning streak to six sessions on Thursday, but more notable was that foreign investors had also recorded five consecutive sessions of net buying across the market. \nFinance \r\n-\r\nThu, August 27, 2026 | 11:40 pm GMT+7\r\nTrungnam Group: Planting the seeds of future generations through schools \r\nWith a people-centered philosophy, Nguyen Tam Thinh, chairman of Trungnam, has expanded the group's education investment efforts from mountainous areas to the plains, completing a number of large-scale school projects. \nCompanies \r\n-\r\nThu, August 27, 2026 | 4:58 pm GMT+7\r\nTasco, PVI Insurance forge multi-sector partnership to deepen ecosystem synergies \r\nTasco Joint Stock Company and PVI Insurance Corporation have expanded their cooperation through a series of agreements, with four Tasco subsidiaries – Tasco Insurance, Savico, VETC Digital and Carpla Service – creating new opportunities across insurance, technology, automotive distribution and after-sales services. \nCompanies \r\n-\r\nThu, August 27, 2026 | 3:51 pm GMT+7\r\nNorthern Vietnam province opens bid for two eco-urban projects worth $491 mln \r\nVietnam’s northern province of Thai Nguyen has launched bidding for two large-scale eco-urban and tourism projects around the Ghenh Che lake, with total investment of VND12.8 trillion ($490.8 million). \nReal Estate \r\n-\r\nThu, August 27, 2026 | 3:36 pm GMT+7\r\nDouble-digit growth: When capital and labor no longer the main drivers \r\nExperts say Vietnam cannot achieve double-digit economic growth by relying solely on capital and labor, but needs new growth engines driven by science, technology, innovation and digital transformation. \nEconomy \r\n-\r\nThu, August 27, 2026 | 2:45 pm GMT+7\r\nF88 maintains growth momentum, ranks among Vietnam's top 100 private tax-paying companies \r\nF88 Business Joint Stock Company, a consumer finance major in Vietnam, paid nearly VND500 billion ($19 million) in taxes in 2025, making it one of the biggest private-sector contributors to the state budget. \nCompanies \r\n-\r\nThu, August 27, 2026 | 1:23 pm GMT+7\r\nFLC Group vice chairman, former Boeing Vietnam CEO Michael Vu Nguyen dies at 71 \r\nMichael Vu Nguyen, vice chairman of Vietnam's FLC Group and former country director of Boeing Vietnam, died on Wednesday in the central Vietnam province of Gia Lai. \nCompanies \r\n-\r\nThu, August 27, 2026 | 11:44 am GMT+7\r\nFTSE opens new capital inflows: Will bank stocks finally 'wake up'? \r\nVietnamese banks, with eight stocks added to the FTSE Emerging All Cap index, could be among the main beneficiaries when the country's upgrade to secondary emerging market status takes effect on September 21. \nFinance \r\n-\r\nThu, August 27, 2026 | 9:34 am GMT+7\r\nTwo foreign banks reportedly in talks to buy at least 15% of Techcombank, shares hit ceiling \r\nShares of Techcombank (HoSE: TCB) hit their daily trading limit on Wednesday after Reuters reported that France's BNP Paribas and South Korea's KB Kookmin Bank were separately in talks to buy at least a 15% stake in the Vietnamese private lender as strategic investors, in a deal that could be worth around $2 billion. \nFinance \r\n-\r\nThu, August 27, 2026 | 12:37 am GMT+7\r\nChina’s Texhong, TCL plan investment expansion in Vietnam \r\nThe Vietnamese government welcomes plans by Chinese textile giant Texhong and electronics maker TCL to expand investment, as the country seeks to attract higher-quality foreign investment in green technology and high-tech manufacturing. \nIndustries \r\n-\r\nWed, August 26, 2026 | 4:33 pm GMT+7\r\nEVN, TKV leaders inspect Vinh Tan 4 power plant, discuss long-term coal supplies \r\nChairman of Vietnam Electricity (EVN) Dang Hoang An and chairman of Vietnam National Coal and Mineral Industries Group (TKV or Vinacomin) Ngo Hoang Ngan led a delegation on Monday to inspect and work at the Vinh Tan 4 Thermal Power Plant in Vinh Hao commune, Lam Dong province. \nCompanies \r\n-\r\nWed, August 26, 2026 | 4:06 pm GMT+7\r\nVinhomes seeks to raise $115 mln through two bond offerings \r\nVingroup's housing arm Vinhomes JSC (HoSE: VHM) plans to issue two bond tranches worth VND3 trillion ($114.9 million) via private placement, as the property developer steps up fundraising for investment projects. \nCompanies \r\n-\r\nWed, August 26, 2026 | 3:25 pm GMT+7\r\nVietnam’s revised oil and gas law set to spur exploration, services demand \r\nVietnam’s amended oil and gas law is expected to accelerate exploration and field development, potentially creating new work opportunities for oilfield service companies as the country seeks to replenish declining domestic production and attract investment into more challenging resources, according to a report by VietCap Securities (VCS). \nEconomy \r\n-\r\nWed, August 26, 2026 | 3:15 pm GMT+7\r\nLoad more \r\nExecutive Talk \r\nBuilding an inclusive culture is never finished: Carlsberg Vietnam exec \r\nGlobal corporations no longer coming to Vietnam because of incentives \r\nVietnam poised for double-digit growth on boom of new industries, reforms: Standard Chartered exec",
