    "content_text": "Rescuers search for survivors after Nepal–China floods kill at least 165\nHundreds remain missing as Nepal urges unity, and China fears ‘major casualties’ in Tibet’s Gyirong County. \nListen (8 mins) \nSave\nShare Rescuers search for survivors after Nepal–China floods kill at least 165 on social media\nShare\nAdd Al Jazeera on Google \nDamaged houses stand precariously near the swollen, debris-choked banks of the Trishuli river after flash floods in Nepal's Nuwakot district. [Niranjan Shrestha/AP Photo] \nBy AFP , AP and Reuters\nPublished On 26 Aug 2026 26 Aug 2026 \n| Updated: 27 Aug 2026 05:57 AM (GMT) Updated: 27 Aug 2026 05:57 AM (GMT) \nRescue workers are scrambling to find survivors after flash floods hit Nepal’s border with China’s Tibet region, killing at least 165 people and sweeping away villages and damaging roads, bridges and power projects.\nPolice in Nepal said late on Wednesday that they had recovered 162 bodies, while authorities in China said at least three people had died in Tibet’s county of Gyirong. Local officials in Gyirong said they feared “major casualties” following the disaster.\nRecommended Stories \nlist of 4 items list 1 of 4 What caused Nepal’s devastating flood damage and how was it contained? \nlist 2 of 4 After the floods of 2025, can we keep 2026 above water? \nlist 3 of 4 Landslide hits bus in northern India, killing 15, as rescue continues \nlist 4 of 4 Rescuers search for survivors amid risk of fresh floods in Afghanistan \nend of list \nNepal’s Ministry of Culture and Tourism said some 579 tourists – more than 400 of them foreigners – were unaccounted for following the flash floods. The missing foreigners included 142 Indians, 34 Australians, and others from Malaysia, the Netherlands, Portugal, South Africa, South Korea, the United Kingdom and the United States.\nNepalese authorities did not provide a figure for residents in affected areas who are still missing, but police spokesman Abi Narayan Kafle said that 28 police personnel were unaccounted for.\nChina’s CCTV state broadcaster reported that 558 people were missing in the region, including 260 foreign nationals.\nSecurity footage captured on the Chinese side of the border at the time of the disaster shows a wall of mud and debris crashing over a building several stories high, engulfing it as dozens of people ran for their lives below.\nThe floodwaters struck villages and towns in the path of two rivers, Bhote Koshi and Trishuli, and television images showed collapsed homes, floating cars, and a metal bridge being washed away.\nPrayash Adhikari, a climate justice activist in Nepal, said the “devastating” flood had wiped out “whole villages”.\nAdvertisement \n“Hundreds of people are missing,” Adhikari told Al Jazeera from Kathmandu, saying the waters had wiped out buildings, schools, bridges and highways.\n‘Swept away by the mud’\nKeshav Prasad Baral, a 68-year-old survivor who is being treated in hospital in Nepal’s Nuwakot district, said his wife is missing in the flood.\n“It was a huge gush of mud coming straight towards us. As it got closer, it kept rising higher and higher. When I saw it enter our home, I tried to grab my wife’s hand and take her to the terrace. But she was swept away by the mud,” Baral told the Reuters news agency.\n“Four or five hours later, a helicopter came to rescue me. My wife is still somewhere beneath the mud. She’s gone.”\nMany people were caught off-guard when the flash floods hit as there was no heavy rainfall, said Kosh Raj Koirala, an editor of Republica Daily, an English newspaper in Nepal’s capital.\n“[People] were at home … several houses have been washed away, swept away by the river,” Koirala told Al Jazeera.\nIn the hard-hit mountainous district of Rasuwa, which has a population of about 50,000, officials said the floodwaters were too high in the areas of Syapru Besi and Timure for rescue helicopters to land.\nDistrict administrator Narendra ‌Pariyar urged those living on the banks of the Bhote Koshi River in the area to seek higher ground, pointing to reports that nearby villages and infrastructure project sites were washed away.\nThe Nepal Electricity Authority said about 430 megawatts of production capacity, including from hydropower and solar facilities, has been damaged. “The disaster has damaged the production, transmission and distribution facilities of the Authority … resulting in disruption of electricity supply,” it said.\nIn neighbouring India, an official announced that Bihar state had begun evacuating people from six districts as they feared the floods would reach those areas.\n‘All-out’ rescue efforts\nNepalese Prime Minister Balendra Shah called for unity and patience.\n“The government takes full responsibility for your rescue, treatment, food and shelter. In times of such a disaster, we all need to stand together,” Shah said in a statement shared on social media.\nHe convened an emergency meeting of the National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Authority and said that “work has started to take necessary steps in coordination with the medical team, scouts, and Red Cross”.\nThe Nepal Army said it had deployed helicopters and disaster response personnel to the affected area. “The work of collecting details of the damage is under way,” it said in a statement.\nAdvertisement \nIn Beijing, Chinese President Xi Jinping urged “all-out” efforts to search ⁠for the missing, and ⁠called for strengthening monitoring and early warning systems to prevent secondary disasters. The official Xinhua news agency said the mudslide hit China’s Gyirong Port, cutting off roads, communications and power in the Shigatse region.\nAl Jazeera’s Katrina Yu, reporting from Hefei City in eastern China, said Nepalese and Chinese officials expect the death toll from the mudslide to rise sharply as rescue teams reach affected areas. Thousands of personnel – including firefighters, police, military and specialist engineering teams with life-detection equipment – have been deployed by both governments, she said, with China also sending military transport planes.\n“There are three major difficulties for reaching those who are missing. The first is access – even on a good day, because of the terrain, it takes a very long time to reach these areas, and this mudslide completely destroyed and collapsed several roads, very important bridges – communication have been cut,” Yu said.\n“The second is that many of these worst-affected areas are still covered by very deep soft mud. It’s very difficult for heavy machinery to go into those areas safely to retrieve and search for people; and thirdly, the weather. Rain is also forecast for the next few days until Sunday, some of it heavy rain over these low-lying areas, and that is expected to further hamper rescue operations.”\nDamaged property and flooded roads following a flash flood in Galchhi, Dhading district, Nepal [Reuters] International aid\nThe US offered its “deepest condolences” to the victims and pledged $500,000 in aid for Nepal for emergency shelter, relief items, water, sanitation and hygiene assistance for communities impacted by the floods.\nThe United Nations also said it was sending supplies and personnel to support affected Nepali communities.\nSouth Korea – which said eight of its citizens working on a hydropower plant were missing – established a ⁠task force led by the foreign minister, and plans to send a joint ‌rapid-response team comprising government officials and fire and police authorities to ‌Nepal ‌as soon as possible.\nIt also said that 10 other workers employed by two companies, Korea ‌Energy and Doosan Enerbility Co, were stranded, adding that Nepal has deployed a rescue team to search the affected area.\nRescue personnel use an excavator to carry a person affected by flash floods at Devghat in Nepal’s Nuwakot district [AFP] The exact cause of the flash flood was not immediately clear.\nClimate experts at the International Centre for Integrated Mountain Development in Nepal say an avalanche from a glacier likely triggered the flooding.\nThe Lhende Khola River, a tributary of the Bhote Koshi River, witnessed maximum flooding on Wednesday, they said.\n“The Lhende Khola has flooded twice in 14 months, this time triggered by an ice-rock avalanche from a glacier on the Nepal side that blocked the river and released a sudden surge downstream,” said Saswata Sanyal, a disaster risk reduction specialist at the climate research group.\nAdvertisement \nThe US Geological Survey initially said it recorded a magnitude 4.4 earthquake at the time of the disaster, but later said it was seismic energy generated by the glacial rock and ice collapsing and the flow of debris.\nThe ⁠Bhote Koshi River originates in Tibet ⁠and empties into Nepal’s Trishuli River, which eventually flows into India as the Gandak River.\nA flood last year in the Bhote Koshi River killed nine people, ‌with 24 missing, and washed away the “Friendship Bridge” linking Nepal and China, disrupting transport and trade for several months. That flood ‌was ‌triggered by the draining of a supraglacial lake in Tibet, a regional climate monitor said.\nMonsoon rains from June to September regularly trigger deadly floods and landslides across South Asia.\nScientists say climate change is increasing the frequency and intensity of extreme weather events in the region.",
