    "content_text": "Evion Group (ASX:EVG) Shares Jump 14% on First Binding Offtake Deal for Maniry Graphite\nSonal Goyal \nSonal Goyal is a business journalist with nearly five years of experience at Kalkine, where she has specialised in reporting on stocks, broker updates, and corporate press releases. With a master’s in finance, she combines financial knowledge with a sharp news sense, allowing her to quickly identify and deliver the most relevant business stories. Over the years, she has built a reputation for clear, accurate, and insightful reporting that keeps readers informed and ahead of market trends. Outside of her professional life, she enjoys exploring new music and travelling.\n25 August 2026 11:32 AM AEST \nShare\nX (Formerly Twitter)\nWhatsapp\nFacebook\nLinkedIn\nTelegram\nEmail This Article\nCopy Link \n‹ \nEVG \n› \nSummarize with AI\nChat GPT \nPerplexity \nStart Your Free Trial Now! \nImage Source: Shutterstock\nYou are reading a free article with opinions that may differ from the recommendation given by Kalkine in its paid research reports. Become a Kalkine member today to get access to our research reports, in-depth technical and fundamental research. Learn more \nStart Your Free Trial Now! \nKey Highlights\nEvion has executed a binding terms sheet with an established German graphite purchaser for a minimum of approximately 10,000 tonnes per annum of natural flake graphite concentrate from Maniry over an initial five-year term.\nThe agreement represents approximately 26% of the Stage 1 production target of approximately 39,000 tonnes per annum, with approximately 74% of Stage 1 output remaining uncommitted.\nPricing is based on market reference prices, supported by a floor price referenced to the project's cost of production and Debt service obligations, designed to provide downside protection.\nThe agreement follows the grant of Mining Permits over all Maniry project areas in July 2026 and the project's recognition by the European Union as a Strategic Project under its Critical Raw Materials Act, the only graphite project in Africa to hold that designation.\nEvion Group NL (ASX:EVG) has advised that it has executed a binding terms sheet with an established German graphite purchaser for the Supply of natural flake graphite concentrate from the company's 100% owned Maniry Graphite Project in southern Madagascar. The company said this is the first binding sales commitment secured for Maniry concentrate. Shares in Evion Group rose 14.29 percent to $0.024 on the ASX following the announcement, up $0.003 on the day.\nTerms of the Agreement\nThe terms sheet is legally binding on execution and sets the principal commercial terms of a proposed five-year offtake agreement, to be replaced by a definitive long-form agreement within six months of signing. It provides for a minimum of approximately 10,000 tonnes per annum of natural flake graphite concentrate for five years from first production, equivalent to approximately 50,000 tonnes of contracted product across the term. This represents approximately 26% of the Stage 1 production target of approximately 39,000 tonnes per annum, with the company deliberately retaining the remaining 74% for discussions with additional customers and strategic partners.\nPricing under the agreement is determined by reference to published market prices for comparable graphite concentrates, normalised to CIF major German ports, together with a floor price referenced to the project's all-in cost of production and its project debt service obligations, with the purchase price for each shipment being the greater of the two. The company said this floor price is designed to provide downside protection and support the long-term commercial viability of Maniry across the term of the agreement.\nPurchaser Profile\nThe purchaser is described by Evion as an established German graphite Business with a long and proven history of supplying and processing natural flake graphite products for industrial end users throughout Europe and international markets. The company said it does not consider the identity of the purchaser to be information that a reasonable person would expect to have a material effect on the price or value of its securities, and that the description provided is sufficient to describe the purchaser's standing and creditworthiness.\nStrategic Context\nThe agreement follows the grant of Mining Permits over all Maniry project areas in July 2026 and the project's recognition by the European Union as a Strategic Project under its Critical Raw Materials Act, which the company said makes it the only graphite project in Africa to hold that designation. The company said completion of the definitive agreement and implementation of the proposed offtake arrangement remains subject to customary conditions precedent, including the company reaching a Final Investment Decision, securing project funding and all necessary approvals, and completing construction and commencing commercial production.\nManagement Commentary\nEvion Managing Director David Round said this is the first binding sales commitment in Maniry's history and represents the milestone that changes the conversation about the project, noting the company has now secured tenure, European Union strategic recognition and a contracted European customer. He said an established German buyer's binding five-year commitment provides independent validation of Maniry concentrate that no study could provide, forming the foundation for project financing discussions. Mr Round added that the roughly three-quarters of Stage 1 production remaining uncommitted, with continued discussions with additional customers and strategic partners, demonstrates Demand for Maniry graphite without committing the project's entire production profile at this stage of development.\nNext Steps\nThe company said next steps include the execution of a definitive long-form offtake agreement within six months, continued engagement with prospective project financiers, continued commercial discussions with additional customers and strategic partners regarding uncommitted Stage 1 production, and advancement of the remaining workstreams required to reach a Final Investment Decision for Maniry.\nConclusion\nEvion Group has secured its first binding sales commitment for the Maniry Graphite Project, addressing one of the principal preconditions to project financing and providing independent validation of product quality from an established European buyer. With approximately a quarter of Stage 1 production now contracted and the remainder available for further commercial discussions, the agreement builds on recent milestones including the grant of Mining Permits and the project's recognition as a European Union Strategic Project. Completion of a definitive long-form agreement and the broader offtake arrangement remain conditional on the company reaching a Final Investment Decision, securing project funding and completing construction.\nFAQs\nQ: What has Evion Group agreed with its German graphite purchaser?\nA: Evion has executed a binding terms sheet for a minimum of approximately 10,000 tonnes per annum of natural flake graphite concentrate from Maniry over an initial five-year term, equivalent to approximately 50,000 tonnes of contracted product.\nQ: What proportion of Maniry's Stage 1 production does this agreement cover?\nA: The agreement covers approximately 26% of the Stage 1 production target of approximately 39,000 tonnes per annum, leaving approximately 74% of Stage 1 output uncommitted for further customers and strategic partners.\nQ: How is pricing determined under the offtake agreement?\nA: Pricing is based on published market reference prices for comparable graphite concentrates, normalised to CIF major German ports, with a floor price referenced to the project's cost of production and debt service obligations, using whichever is greater for each shipment.\nQ: Is the offtake agreement finalised?\nA: No. The terms sheet is legally binding on execution but sets out the principal commercial terms only; it is to be replaced by a definitive long-form agreement within six months, and the arrangement remains subject to conditions including a Final Investment Decision and securing project funding.\nQ: What recognition has the Maniry Graphite Project received from the European Union?\nA: Maniry has been recognised by the European Union as a Strategic Project under its Critical Raw Materials Act, which the company said makes it the only graphite project in Africa to hold that designation.\nDownload Free Report – Explore 3 Stock Ideas & Industry Insights\nUnlock 3 stock ideas and key industry insights in our free report. This information is general in nature and does not consider your personal objectives, financial situation, or needs. It is not financial advice.\nAll investments involve risk—consider independent advice before making any investment decisions.\nView 3 Research Reports \nDisclaimer: \nThis article (&ldquo;Article&rdquo;) has been prepared by Kalkine Pty Limited (ABN 34 154 808 312) (Australian financial services licence number 425376) (&ldquo;Kalkine&rdquo;) and its related bodies corporate who are authorised to provide general financial product advice. Kalkine.com.au and its associated pages are published by Kalkine.\nAny information/advice provided in this article is general in nature and does not take into account your objectives, financial situation or needs. You should therefore consider whether the information is appropriate for your objectives, financial situation and needs before acting upon it.\nThere may be a Product Disclosure Statement, Information Memorandum or other offer document (&ldquo;Offer Document &rdquo; ) for the securities or other financial products referred to in Kalkine articles. You should obtain a copy of the Offer Document and consider it before making any decision about whether to acquire the security or financial product.