    "content_text": "Vestas (CPSE:VWS) Stock Trades At A Cash Flow Discount But Earnings Look Fair\nBailey Pemberton \nAugust 23, 2026 4 min read \nVWS.CO \n-1.57% \nVWS \nFind winning stocks in any market cycle. Join 7 million investors using Simply Wall St's investing ideas for FREE. \nVestas Wind Systems stock has delivered a 51.9% return over the past year, while the current valuation picture is mixed, with the Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) estimate pointing to around 23.4% upside relative to the share price. \nOver the last 12 months, Vestas Wind Systems has returned 51.9%, which puts added focus on whether the current price already reflects improved expectations. \nRecent news around higher profit expectations, a €400 million share buyback and new turbine orders can support investor confidence. However, execution risk on large projects and the capital required for growth may still weigh on how much value the market is willing to assign. \nThe company scores 4 out of 6 on the valuation checks , which points to a mixed picture rather than a clear bargain or clear overvaluation. \nFor investors, the debate is whether Vestas Wind Systems stock now prices in most of the recent optimism or if the DCF based intrinsic value still leaves a reasonable margin of upside. \nVestas Wind Systems delivered 51.9% returns over the last year. See how this stacks up to the rest of the Electrical industry. \nDoes Vestas Wind Systems Look Undervalued on Cash Flow? \nThe Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) approach here looks at the cash Vestas Wind Systems could generate for shareholders over time. The model uses latest twelve month free cash flow of about €973 million and assumes that cash flows continue growing rather than shrinking, which suits a business tied to ongoing turbine projects and service contracts. \nOn these assumptions, the DCF model points to an estimated intrinsic value of around DKK272 per share. That is above the current share price, implying the stock appears about 23.4% undervalued on this cash flow view. Because the recent profit guidance upgrade and €400 million buyback have already lifted sentiment, the implied discount suggests the market is still not fully crediting the projected cash generation. \nOverall, the cash flow based valuation indicates Vestas Wind Systems stock appears undervalued relative to the intrinsic value implied by the DCF model. \nOur Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) analysis suggests Vestas Wind Systems is undervalued by 23.4%. Track this in your watchlist or portfolio , or discover 267 more high quality undervalued stocks . \nVWS Discounted Cash Flow as at Aug 2026 Head to the Valuation section of our Company Report for more details on how we arrive at this Fair Value for Vestas Wind Systems. \nStory Continues \nDoes Vestas Wind Systems Look Fairly Valued on Earnings? \nP/E is a useful yardstick for Vestas Wind Systems because earnings remain a key focus for investors in capital goods and equipment stocks. \nThe stock trades on a P/E of about 24.8x, which is below the Electrical industry average of roughly 29.6x and also below the peer group average near 49.2x. On Simply Wall St's fair P/E estimate of about 24.6x, which reflects Vestas Wind Systems' specific earnings profile, risk and size, the current multiple sits very close to what the model suggests as a reasonable level. \nThis indicates a market view that already reflects the recent improvement in sentiment, without pushing the earnings multiple far above what the company profile would typically support. The valuation on P/E does not appear clearly cheap or clearly expensive compared with sector norms and the tailored fair ratio. \nOn the P/E yardstick, Vestas Wind Systems stock appears roughly fairly valued at current levels. \nCPSE:VWS P/E Ratio as at Aug 2026 See what the numbers say about this price — find out in our valuation breakdown. \nThe Vestas Wind Systems Narrative: What Would Justify Today's Price? \nSimply Wall St Narratives pick up where the valuation puzzle for Vestas Wind Systems leaves off and clarify which paths for growth, margins and earnings would need to occur for the stock to be worth materially more or less than today's price. Each one presents Vestas Wind Systems' fair value as a thesis about the business that you can revisit over time, rather than a single snapshot, and they sit on the company's Community page. \nCommunity views on Vestas Wind Systems are split between a higher conviction upside case and a more cautious, execution risk focused stance. \nBull case: 17% undervalued \n\"Vestas' technology leadership, including next-generation modular turbines and integrated digital optimization platforms, positions the company to capture outsized share in high-growth, high-margin segments…\" \nRead the full Bull Case to see why Vestas Wind Systems could be undervalued \nBear case: 11% overvalued \n\"Intensifying price competition in Europe from Chinese wind turbine manufacturers is highlighted as a risk, which could result in further margin compression and threaten Vestas's ability to sustain profitable growth…\" \nRead the full Bear Case to see why Vestas Wind Systems could be overvalued \nDo you think there's more to the story for Vestas Wind Systems? Head over to our Community to see what others are saying! \nThe Bottom Line \nFor Vestas Wind Systems, the Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) work still points to meaningful intrinsic value upside, while the P/E based view looks about right compared with peers. That mix of an undervalued cash flow signal and a more neutral earnings multiple fits a broader picture that is neither clearly cheap nor clearly stretched. \nThe real question now is whether Vestas Wind Systems can convert its project pipeline into durable cash generation without profit setbacks or overruns. If execution on large contracts and capital spending stays under control, the DCF style upside case has more support. If not, the current valuation could prove to be the market correctly pricing that risk. \nThis article by Simply Wall St is general in nature. We provide commentary based on historical data and analyst forecasts only using an unbiased methodology and our articles are not intended to be financial advice. It does not constitute a recommendation to buy or sell any stock, and does not take account of your objectives, or your financial situation. We aim to bring you long-term focused analysis driven by fundamental data. Note that our analysis may not factor in the latest price-sensitive company announcements or qualitative material. Simply Wall St has no position in any stocks mentioned. \nCompanies discussed in this article include VWS.CO . \nHave feedback on this article? Concerned about the content? Get in touch with us directly. Alternatively, email editorial-team@simplywallst.com \nView Comments \nTerms and Privacy Policy \nPrivacy Dashboard \nMore Info",
