    "content_text": "Thermo Fisher (TMO) Stock Looks Fully Valued Following Its Microbiology Sale\nBailey Pemberton \nAugust 22, 2026 4 min read \nTMO \n-1.04% \nFind your next quality investment with Simply Wall St's easy and powerful screener, trusted by over 7 million individual investors worldwide. \nThermo Fisher Scientific stock has delivered a strong 26.3% return over the past year, yet the valuation checks suggest the shares are no longer an obvious bargain. The Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) estimate points to a level that is close to the current price, while market multiples lean richer. \nOver the past 12 months Thermo Fisher Scientific has returned 26.3%, which puts more pressure on today's buyers to justify paying up at current levels. \nParticipation in areas such as protein design tools and partnerships in proteomics and precision medicine can support expectations for long term cash flows. At the same time, portfolio reshaping like the sale of the microbiology business may introduce execution risk if growth and returns from the remaining units do not meet investor hopes. \nThermo Fisher Scientific scores 2 out of 6 on the broader valuation checks , which leans expensive rather than a clear bargain, despite the DCF suggesting the stock is roughly fairly valued. \nThe issue now is whether Thermo Fisher Scientific's current share price already reflects most of the value that the business can reasonably justify, or if there is still room for further upside without stretching valuation too far. \nThermo Fisher Scientific delivered 26.3% returns over the last year. See how this stacks up to the rest of the Life Sciences industry. \nDoes Thermo Fisher Scientific Look Fairly Valued on Cash Flow? \nThe Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) model values Thermo Fisher Scientific by projecting the cash it can return to shareholders over time and discounting it back to today. On this basis, the company's latest twelve month free cash flow of about $7.4b supports an estimated intrinsic value of roughly $634 per share, which is very close to the current market price. \nThe projections behind this DCF reflect growing free cash flows rather than a turnaround story, which is consistent with a mature, cash generative business. The implied discount of about 0.8% points to Thermo Fisher Scientific stock being only marginally undervalued rather than a clear mispricing. Recent headlines around the sale of the microbiology business and ongoing work in protein design and proteomics help explain why the share price already tracks the DCF estimate so tightly. \nOverall, Thermo Fisher Scientific appears roughly fairly valued on this DCF view, with only a slight tilt toward being undervalued. \nStory Continues \nThermo Fisher Scientific is fairly valued according to our Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) , but this can change at a moment's notice. Track the value in your watchlist or portfolio and be alerted on when to act. \nTMO Discounted Cash Flow as at Aug 2026 Head to the Valuation section of our Company Report for more details on how we arrive at this Fair Value for Thermo Fisher Scientific. \nHas Thermo Fisher Scientific Run Too Far on Earnings? \nThe P/E ratio is a useful way to look at Thermo Fisher Scientific because earnings are a key driver of how investors usually value large, established life sciences companies. \nThermo Fisher Scientific trades on a P/E of about 33.4x. That is almost in line with the peer average of 33.1x and below the broader Life Sciences industry average of 39.1x. On simpler comparisons the stock does not screen as an extreme outlier on earnings relative to its sector. \nHowever, a more tailored fair P/E multiple for Thermo Fisher Scientific, which factors in its growth profile, margins, scale and risk, is estimated at about 28.5x. The current 33.4x therefore represents a premium to this fair ratio, indicating that investors are paying more for the earnings today compared with what this framework would support. \nOn this P/E lens, Thermo Fisher Scientific stock appears expensive relative to the earnings multiple that the fair ratio would imply. \nNYSE:TMO P/E Ratio as at Aug 2026 See what the numbers say about this price — find out in our valuation breakdown. \nThe Thermo Fisher Scientific Narrative: What Would Justify Today's Price? \nSimply Wall St Narratives for Thermo Fisher Scientific connect the valuation picture above to clear assumptions about future growth, margins and earnings that would need to hold for the stock to trade meaningfully higher or lower than it does today. These narratives sit on the company's Community page. Each one anchors a fair value to a specific story about Thermo Fisher Scientific's possible catalysts and risks so you can track which scenario appears to be unfolding over time. \nThermo Fisher Scientific attracts sharply different views, with some community members seeing resilient growth drivers while others focus on structural and geopolitical risks. \nBull case: roughly fairly valued \n\"Strong momentum in high-impact innovation evidenced by next-generation analytical tools like the Orbitrap mass spectrometers and AI integration into drug development workflows positions Thermo Fisher to capture incremental share as genomics and precision medicine proliferate…\" \nRead the full Bull Case to see why Thermo Fisher Scientific could be undervalued \nBear case: 18% overvalued \n\"Accelerating geopolitical tensions and trade restrictions, especially between the U.S. and China, are expected to significantly disrupt Thermo Fisher's global supply chains, create persistent cost headwinds, and limit growth opportunities in key international markets, thereby putting long-term pressure on both revenue and net margins…\" \nRead the full Bear Case to see why Thermo Fisher Scientific could be overvalued \nDo you think there's more to the story for Thermo Fisher Scientific? Head over to our Community to see what others are saying! \nThe Bottom Line \nThermo Fisher Scientific looks roughly in line with its Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) intrinsic value estimate, which suggests the stock is no longer obviously cheap. By contrast, the tailored P/E multiple points to the shares trading on an overvalued earnings basis, and the broader valuation checks also lean weak. The tension between these frameworks reflects solid cash generation on one side and fuller growth expectations and sentiment on the other. The key question from here is whether Thermo Fisher Scientific can deliver the growth, margins and execution on portfolio changes that keep investors comfortable paying this richer earnings multiple. \nThis article by Simply Wall St is general in nature. We provide commentary based on historical data and analyst forecasts only using an unbiased methodology and our articles are not intended to be financial advice. It does not constitute a recommendation to buy or sell any stock, and does not take account of your objectives, or your financial situation. We aim to bring you long-term focused analysis driven by fundamental data. Note that our analysis may not factor in the latest price-sensitive company announcements or qualitative material. Simply Wall St has no position in any stocks mentioned. \nCompanies discussed in this article include TMO . \nHave feedback on this article? Concerned about the content? Get in touch with us directly. Alternatively, email editorial-team@simplywallst.com \nView Comments \nTerms and Privacy Policy \nYour privacy controls \nWA Consumer Health Privacy Policy \nMore Info",
