    "content_text": "Cell therapy weekly: FDA approves glycogen storage disease type 1a gene therapy\n20 Aug 2026 \r\nWritten by Kadeja Johnson \r\nDisease area \r\nFDA \r\nGene therapy \r\nNews \r\nRegulation & policy \r\nThe week in cell therapy \r\nThis week: Sangamo Therapeutics (CA, USA) auctions off its main assets, Andelyn Biosciences (OH, USA) and Queen’s University (Ontario, Canada) partner to advance a lysosomal storage disorder gene therapy program and the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA; MD, USA) approves a gene therapy for glycogen storage disease type 1a (GSD1a).\nThe news highlights:\nSangamo completes auctions of main assets \nPartnership to advance gene therapy program for lysosomal storage disorder \nFDA approves glycogen storage disease type 1a gene therapy \nSangamo completes auctions of main assets\nIn June 2026, Sangamo Therapeutics announced plans to sell its assets through voluntary Chapter 11 bankruptcy proceedings to maximize stakeholder value. Nearly two months later, the company has successfully concluded the auction process, selecting winning bids totaling approximately US$163.55 million in cash at closing, plus up to US$100 million in potential future milestone payments.\nDuring a court-supervised auction process:\nPTC Therapeutics (NJ, USA) acquired Sangamo’s Fabry disease program, ST-920, for US$111 million cash, plus up to US$100 million in milestone payments upon receiving traditional or full approval from the FDA.\nEli Lilly and Company (IN, USA) purchased Sangamo’s capsid delivery, zinc finger and modular integrase platforms, plus the prion disease program, ST-506, for US$50 million cash.\nVarious bidders secured Sangamo tools and equipment for US$2.55 million cash considerations at closing\n“Reaching the conclusion of this first competitive auction represents an important milestone in Sangamo’s reorganization,” said Sandy Macrae, CEO of Sangamo. “Our priority has been to seek value-maximizing transactions for all stakeholders, and we are pleased that our platform technologies, Fabry disease and prion programs attracted strong interest from parties who have the scale to continue their development for patients in need. We remain focused on monetizing our remaining assets and seeking Court approvals to finalize all sales.”\nRead more \nPartnership to advance gene therapy program for lysosomal storage disorder\nAndelyn Biosciences and Queen’s University have partnered to develop and manufacture AAV9-GM2, a gene therapy indicated for GM2 gangliosidosis. GM2 gangliosidosis is a rare, inherited lysosomal storage disorder, progressively destroying nerve cells in the brain and spinal cord. GM2 is caused by genetic mutations that disrupt normal lysosomal function, leading to the buildup of harmful substances in cells and severe decline in neurological abilities.\nThis partnership aims to advance AAV9-GM2 by leveraging Andelyn’s AAV Curator® Platform. Commenting on the partnership, Matt Niloff, Andelyn’s Chief Commercial Officer, spoke on how the company is “proud to partner with Queen’s University to advance this gene therapy candidate for the benefit of patients and families facing the challenges of GM2 gangliosidoses.”\nContinuing, Niloff reflected on the company’s commitment to support innovative gene therapy programs targeting rare and ultra-rare diseases, stating:\n“Our deep expertise in AAV development and production allows us to support Queen’s University with the scale and quality rigor necessary to bring this life-changing therapy one step closer to reality.”\nRead more \nFDA approves glycogen storage disease type 1a gene therapy\nThe FDA has granted accelerated approval for GENGLYCOS™ – a gene therapy developed by Ultragenyx Pharmaceuticals (CA, USA) – in adult and pediatric patients eight years and older with GSD1a. GSD1a, an ultra-rare genetic metabolic disorder, is caused by a deficiency of the enzyme glucose-6-phosphatase-alpha, reducing the liver’s ability to release glucose into the bloodstream. Patients with this disorder require nutritional management consisting of raw cornstarch intake as an oral glucose replacement therapy.\n“The approval of GENGLYCOS fulfills our commitment to provide the first therapy that directly targets the root cause of GSD1a. The reduced reliance on cornstarch, experienced by patients in our clinical studies, demonstrates this gene therapy’s ability to establish the normal breakdown of glycogen to produce glucose during fasting or episodes of metabolic stress. This ability to regulate glucose has alleviated the disease burden and has the potential to mitigate the risk of severe or life-threatening hypoglycemia for these patients,” commented Ultragenyx’ chief medical offer, Eric Crombez. “As our first gene therapy approval, GENGLYCOS represents an important achievement for our company and the realization of the promise of a powerful new tool to deliver transformative medicines for people living with rare diseases.”\nRead more \nPrevious article \r\nNext article \r\nRelated articles\n14 MAR 2024 \r\nCELL THERAPY \r\nCell therapy weekly: Transforming organ transport at cryogenic temperatures \r\n14 JUL 2022 \r\nCELL THERAPY \r\nCell therapy weekly: Vertex acquires ViaCyte to accelerate development of stem cell-derived islet cell replacement therapy \r\n18 DEC 2020 \r\nINDUSTRY DEVELOPMENTS \r\nIndustry updates with Dusko Ilic: September 2020 \r\n8 SEP 2023 \r\nTHE WEEK IN CELL THERAPY \r\nCell therapy weekly: Kriya launches gene therapy program for non-alcoholic steatohepatitis \r\n19 FEB 2024 \r\nCLINICAL MANUFACTURING \r\nCell therapy weekly: Gene-edited porcine donors for xenotransplantation \r\n17 MAR 2022 \r\nCELL THERAPY \r\nCell therapy weekly: Mayo Clinic finds disparity in the quality of genomic data across racial groups",
