    "content_text": "Umicore reports profitable H1\nThe nonferrous metals smelter and refinery operator says its Recycling Business Group “delivered an outstanding performance” in the first half of this year.\nUmicore says its first half 2026 profits were bolstered by “a highly supportive metal price environment.”\nPhoto courtesy of Umicore Group \r\nBy\r\nBrian Taylor, Senior Editor \r\nPublished August 04, 2026\r\nListen to article \r\n/ \r\nBelgium-based metals producer and recycler Umicore Group says its adjusted earnings per share (EPS) rose by more than 14 percent in the first half of 2026 compared with the same time frame in 2025.\nThe company, with a recycling business unit that converts end-of-life electronics, jewelry and other items into marketable gold, silver and platinum group metals (PGMs), credits those operations as having “delivered an outstanding performance” from January through June of this year.\nUmicore says the business unit—one of four it operates—increased its adjusted earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortization (EBITDA) by 36 percent year on year on revenue that rose by 8 percent.\n“This was supported by high activity levels overall and particularly in jewelry and industrial metals, with robust demand across its product businesses and a highly supportive metal price environment,” Umicore says of the Recycling business group’s first half of 2026.\nThe firm adds, “At the core of Umicore’s closed-loop metals business model, the Recycling Business Group demonstrated once again its ability to capture favorable market dynamics. It continues to improve efficiency, optimize its business model and support sustainable value creation.”\nLooking at the rest of 2026, Umicore &nbsp;says, “Recycling’s adjusted EBITDA for FY 2026 is expected to be significantly above FY 2025 [although it is] expected to be lower in the second half of 2026 compared with” the first half.\nAccording to its first half 2026 income statement, Umicore’s Recycling Business Group accounted for nearly 75 percent of the company’s total sales for the period.\nLatest from Recycling Today \nScrap Expo 2026: Buddy CEO releases playbook for the modern scrapyard \nArmor Equipment acquires Dakota Truck \nScrap Expo 2026: Ferrous markets in transition \nNippon Steel announces EAF investment in Europe \nRail shipper group to meet in November \nNucor, SDI calculate healthy summer profits \nGreyparrot launches dashboard to measure equipment effectiveness \nNew books offer RIOS, R2 insight \nMore from Recycling Today \nGalbreath offers upgraded container handler \nBritish, Australian steel sectors remain in flux \nCRI schedules DRS antifraud webinar \nKadant announces CEO succession plan \nPCPA releases design guide for poly-coated paper packaging \nMassachusetts awards recycling, reuse grants to 16 businesses",
